1
00:00:00,560 --> 00:00:03,740
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:04,020 --> 00:00:04,420
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:05,040 --> 00:00:05,540
My name is Catherine.

4
00:00:06,160 --> 00:00:09,780
And as you know, we've had a series going about small talk in English.

5
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:12,420
So today's lesson is a part of that series.

6
00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:13,020
That's right.

7
00:00:13,020 --> 00:00:19,260
Today we're going to listen to two friends, two girlfriends meeting and they haven't seen each other for a long time.

8
00:00:19,260 --> 00:00:22,700
So we're just going to see how they quickly have a small conversation.

9
00:00:28,650 --> 00:00:30,350
Hey Michelle, is that you?

10
00:00:30,610 --> 00:00:33,030
Wow, Grace, long time no see.

11
00:00:33,790 --> 00:00:34,610
Where have you been?

12
00:00:35,110 --> 00:00:40,410
Oh yeah, well, you see I got a promotion so I moved to the new Pudong office last September.

13
00:00:40,750 --> 00:00:41,270
You did?

14
00:00:41,770 --> 00:00:42,130
Congratulations.

15
00:00:43,070 --> 00:00:43,710
Thanks a lot.

16
00:00:43,990 --> 00:00:45,730
So how are things with you, Michelle?

17
00:00:46,330 --> 00:00:49,850
Well, same old, same old, you know, nothing much has changed here.

18
00:00:50,150 --> 00:00:51,050
Are you still seeing Chris?

19
00:00:51,690 --> 00:00:53,950
No, actually we split up last month.

20
00:00:54,450 --> 00:00:56,590
Oh dear, I'm sorry to hear that.

21
00:00:57,640 --> 00:01:00,740
But I met a really cute guy last night at a networking party.

22
00:01:01,120 --> 00:01:03,300
So well, let's just wait and see.

23
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:04,360
Good for you, Michelle.

24
00:01:08,220 --> 00:01:09,060
All right, we're back.

25
00:01:09,280 --> 00:01:15,240
So now let's take a look at some of the language on Language Takeaway.

26
00:01:15,240 --> 00:01:15,560
Language Takeaway.

27
00:01:17,640 --> 00:01:20,750
So the first word we have here today, Marco, is the word promotion.

28
00:01:21,760 --> 00:01:27,520
Grace says, well, you see, I got a promotion so I moved to the new Pudong office.

29
00:01:27,520 --> 00:01:27,710
That's right.

30
00:01:28,520 --> 00:01:31,990
So she got a promotion and a promotion is work related.

31
00:01:32,700 --> 00:01:34,470
That means that you got a better position.

32
00:01:35,160 --> 00:01:36,160
That's right.

33
00:01:36,160 --> 00:01:36,670
But you didn't change companies.

34
00:01:37,570 --> 00:01:41,330
So in your company, you had a job and you did well in that job.

35
00:01:41,370 --> 00:01:44,620
And so your boss gave you a better job in the company.

36
00:01:44,800 --> 00:01:46,120
And that's a promotion.

37
00:01:46,120 --> 00:01:46,300
That's right.

38
00:01:47,120 --> 00:01:48,680
Okay, so she got a promotion.

39
00:01:49,200 --> 00:01:59,320
Grace, when they were talking about work and now they started talking about their personal life and Grace asked if Michelle is still seeing Chris.

40
00:01:59,690 --> 00:02:03,810
Okay, so the verb here is to see someone, to be seeing someone.

41
00:02:04,190 --> 00:02:06,530
We're not actually talking about eyes and looking.

42
00:02:06,960 --> 00:02:08,520
We're talking about dating.

43
00:02:08,520 --> 00:02:08,690
That's right.

44
00:02:09,520 --> 00:02:12,030
So if you're seeing Chris, it means you are dating Chris.

45
00:02:12,760 --> 00:02:13,760
Okay.

46
00:02:13,760 --> 00:02:17,330
So that's an interesting way of describing a relationship.

47
00:02:17,720 --> 00:02:20,410
So you say, yeah, I'm sorry, I'm seeing someone.

48
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:21,850
I can't date you.

49
00:02:21,970 --> 00:02:22,890
I'm seeing someone else.

50
00:02:23,190 --> 00:02:25,990
Or you could ask someone, are you seeing anyone right now?

51
00:02:26,860 --> 00:02:27,920
That means are you dating anyone?

52
00:02:28,020 --> 00:02:29,120
Do you have a boyfriend basically?

53
00:02:29,940 --> 00:02:30,340
Okay.

54
00:02:30,880 --> 00:02:33,520
And sadly, no, they are not together anymore.

55
00:02:33,820 --> 00:02:36,840
She says, actually, we split up last month.

56
00:02:37,080 --> 00:02:37,360
All right.

57
00:02:38,080 --> 00:02:39,480
So this is another relationship term.

58
00:02:39,480 --> 00:02:43,240
To split up means to break up or to stop dating.

59
00:02:43,840 --> 00:02:45,130
To stop seeing each other.

60
00:02:45,880 --> 00:02:50,390
So there are two ways of seeing it, as you said, to split up or to break up.

61
00:02:50,960 --> 00:02:51,960
That's right.

62
00:02:51,960 --> 00:02:54,550
So unfortunately, they split up or unfortunately, they broke up.

63
00:02:54,750 --> 00:02:56,610
They are no longer seeing each other.

64
00:02:56,890 --> 00:02:57,490
That's right.

65
00:02:58,240 --> 00:02:59,240
Okay.

66
00:02:59,240 --> 00:03:04,930
But she said she had some good news because she met a really cute guy at a networking party.

67
00:03:05,440 --> 00:03:06,440
What is networking?

68
00:03:06,440 --> 00:03:07,440
All right.

69
00:03:07,440 --> 00:03:11,400
So networking is something that you do, especially in the business world.

70
00:03:11,400 --> 00:03:15,720
You go to events, parties, happy hours, and you meet people.

71
00:03:15,720 --> 00:03:21,680
You meet strangers and you talk about work and you try to meet as many people as you can.

72
00:03:21,680 --> 00:03:22,680
Okay.

73
00:03:22,680 --> 00:03:26,760
So it's not really a social party to actually just go and meet random people.

74
00:03:26,760 --> 00:03:32,200
You're trying to meet people that will be interesting for your business or maybe to get some other ideas.

75
00:03:32,200 --> 00:03:33,720
It's for a business purpose.

76
00:03:33,720 --> 00:03:34,720
That's right.

77
00:03:34,720 --> 00:03:42,120
So perhaps in your town, you own a small business and your town has decided to have a chamber of commerce networking party.

78
00:03:42,120 --> 00:03:44,330
So you can meet other small business owners.

79
00:03:44,720 --> 00:03:45,050
Okay.

80
00:03:45,050 --> 00:03:45,650
Very good.

81
00:03:46,230 --> 00:03:48,090
So that's what we have for language takeaway.

82
00:03:48,590 --> 00:03:51,030
Let's go back and listen to our dialogue for the second time.

83
00:03:52,320 --> 00:03:55,600
Hey, Michelle.

84
00:03:57,980 --> 00:03:58,480
Is that you?

85
00:03:58,760 --> 00:03:59,740
Wow, Grace.

86
00:04:00,400 --> 00:04:02,280
Long time no see.

87
00:04:02,280 --> 00:04:02,740
Where have you been?

88
00:04:03,280 --> 00:04:03,700
Oh yeah.

89
00:04:04,040 --> 00:04:08,560
Well, you see, I got a promotion so I moved to the new Pudong office last September.

90
00:04:08,900 --> 00:04:09,400
You did?

91
00:04:09,940 --> 00:04:10,320
Congratulations.

92
00:04:11,220 --> 00:04:11,860
Thanks a lot.

93
00:04:12,140 --> 00:04:13,840
So how are things with you, Michelle?

94
00:04:14,580 --> 00:04:18,000
Well, same old, same old, you know, nothing much has changed here.

95
00:04:18,280 --> 00:04:19,160
Are you still seeing Chris?

96
00:04:19,960 --> 00:04:22,100
No, actually we split up last month.

97
00:04:22,580 --> 00:04:23,060
Oh dear.

98
00:04:23,540 --> 00:04:24,720
I'm sorry to hear that.

99
00:04:25,720 --> 00:04:28,880
But I met a really cute guy last night at a networking party.

100
00:04:29,200 --> 00:04:31,420
So well, let's just wait and see.

101
00:04:31,680 --> 00:04:32,500
Good for you, Michelle.

102
00:04:38,800 --> 00:04:39,800
All right.

103
00:04:39,800 --> 00:04:45,130
Let's talk about these small, quick but very useful phrases for small talk on Fluency Builder.

104
00:04:46,920 --> 00:04:47,180
Fluency Builder.

105
00:04:48,280 --> 00:04:56,180
So this first phrase is a phrase that you'll hear a lot when people greet each other but perhaps friends who haven't seen each other in a very long time.

106
00:04:56,400 --> 00:04:59,040
We use this phrase, long time no see.

107
00:04:59,260 --> 00:04:59,700
That's right.

108
00:05:00,560 --> 00:05:04,480
Now, you look at it and it may seem like it's incorrect, right?

109
00:05:04,480 --> 00:05:06,560
Because you're missing something.

110
00:05:06,560 --> 00:05:07,560
That's right.

111
00:05:07,560 --> 00:05:07,910
Long time no see.

112
00:05:08,560 --> 00:05:08,870
Who no see?

113
00:05:09,560 --> 00:05:10,560
What long time?

114
00:05:10,560 --> 00:05:10,750
That's right.

115
00:05:11,560 --> 00:05:14,190
But this actually means I haven't seen you in a long time.

116
00:05:14,800 --> 00:05:15,030
That's right.

117
00:05:15,800 --> 00:05:17,690
So the phrase is long time no see.

118
00:05:18,160 --> 00:05:20,320
Hey, Michelle, long time no see.

119
00:05:20,320 --> 00:05:20,690
How are you?

120
00:05:21,310 --> 00:05:22,870
That's the way that you use it.

121
00:05:23,030 --> 00:05:24,650
But just take it as a phrase.

122
00:05:24,830 --> 00:05:26,090
Don't try to analyze it too much.

123
00:05:26,410 --> 00:05:27,950
So hey, Marco, long time no see.

124
00:05:28,440 --> 00:05:28,960
That's right.

125
00:05:28,960 --> 00:05:29,580
Okay.

126
00:05:30,160 --> 00:05:32,620
And well, they're talking and she's like, well, how are you?

127
00:05:32,880 --> 00:05:35,780
And she says, well, you know, same old, same old.

128
00:05:36,180 --> 00:05:36,680
All right.

129
00:05:36,760 --> 00:05:37,440
This is another one.

130
00:05:37,540 --> 00:05:38,360
It doesn't look right.

131
00:05:39,040 --> 00:05:40,040
It doesn't look wrong.

132
00:05:40,040 --> 00:05:43,080
But we do say this and twice, same old, same old.

133
00:05:43,600 --> 00:05:45,580
And this means nothing has changed.

134
00:05:46,670 --> 00:05:48,430
There's nothing really new in my life.

135
00:05:48,640 --> 00:05:49,640
Right.

136
00:05:49,640 --> 00:05:50,710
The same old things in my life.

137
00:05:51,160 --> 00:05:52,470
Nothing really new or exciting.

138
00:05:53,040 --> 00:05:53,250
Same old.

139
00:05:54,040 --> 00:05:55,040
Exactly.

140
00:05:55,040 --> 00:05:56,960
So I could say, hey, Marco, how's the job going?

141
00:05:56,960 --> 00:05:58,090
You know, same old, same old.

142
00:05:58,590 --> 00:05:58,890
All right.

143
00:05:59,440 --> 00:06:00,670
So nothing has changed in his job.

144
00:06:00,810 --> 00:06:02,170
He has not gotten a promotion.

145
00:06:02,470 --> 00:06:02,790
That's right.

146
00:06:03,010 --> 00:06:12,070
So now, Michelle was again describing about the guy that she met at the networking party and she said, well, let's just wait and see.

147
00:06:12,430 --> 00:06:15,710
It seems like an incomplete sentence because let's wait and see what.

148
00:06:16,330 --> 00:06:16,750
All right.

149
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:17,930
So let's wait and see what?

150
00:06:18,200 --> 00:06:18,910
That's the question, though.

151
00:06:19,210 --> 00:06:20,430
You know, what are we waiting to see?

152
00:06:20,790 --> 00:06:30,430
Well, she's basically saying we don't know how things will go, but we, you know, we'll have to wait for the future to come before we can make a decision or figure out what's going on.

153
00:06:30,670 --> 00:06:31,030
That's right.

154
00:06:31,960 --> 00:06:35,600
So, for example, Marco, we're at the office and I say, how is that report coming along?

155
00:06:35,600 --> 00:06:37,290
Is it, you know, were the numbers good?

156
00:06:37,590 --> 00:06:41,430
And I can say, yeah, the numbers seemed all right, but we'll just wait and see.

157
00:06:41,630 --> 00:06:46,090
Perhaps we don't have all the numbers yet, so we have to wait to get the rest of them.

158
00:06:46,250 --> 00:06:46,590
That's right.

159
00:06:47,440 --> 00:06:51,800
So you're just basically saying I don't know what will happen in the future.

160
00:06:51,800 --> 00:06:55,330
Let's just hope that it will be good and let's see what happens.

161
00:06:56,040 --> 00:06:56,890
Let's just wait and see.

162
00:06:57,360 --> 00:06:58,230
Let's just wait and see.

163
00:06:58,510 --> 00:06:58,890
All right.

164
00:06:59,110 --> 00:07:01,870
Let's go back and listen to our dialogue one last time.

165
00:07:10,840 --> 00:07:40,160
And guy last night at a networking party.

166
00:07:40,160 --> 00:07:42,340
So well, let's just wait and see.

167
00:07:42,680 --> 00:07:43,400
Good for you, Michelle.

168
00:07:47,870 --> 00:07:50,770
Okay so this was another part of our small talk series.

169
00:07:50,980 --> 00:08:01,660
As you see, very useful and quick little phrases so you can have a very brief conversation with maybe someone you haven't seen or a little chit chat at the office.

170
00:08:02,060 --> 00:08:07,200
That's right and these are things that if you remember them, you memorize them, you can use in almost any conversation.

171
00:08:08,180 --> 00:08:11,940
Also I'm sorry to hear that or good for you.

172
00:08:12,180 --> 00:08:15,160
These are really great phrases that are very, very flexible.

173
00:08:15,720 --> 00:08:15,960
That's right.

174
00:08:16,720 --> 00:08:17,580
Actually that's a really good one.

175
00:08:17,880 --> 00:08:18,920
I'm sorry to hear that.

176
00:08:19,080 --> 00:08:24,340
Maybe someone gives you a little bit of bad news or something bad happened in their life.

177
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:28,460
It's very polite and a nice way of saying hey, I understand.

178
00:08:28,840 --> 00:08:30,020
I'm sorry to hear that.

179
00:08:30,300 --> 00:08:30,820
That's right.

180
00:08:31,100 --> 00:08:33,340
So we hope that you've learned something new in today's lesson.

181
00:08:33,400 --> 00:08:42,000
If you have any questions about the phrases or words you've heard, please check out our website EnglishPod.com and leave us a question on the community section or in a note.

182
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:43,020
We'll be happy to answer them.

183
00:08:43,280 --> 00:08:44,280
Alright guys, we'll see you there.

184
00:08:44,280 --> 00:08:45,280
Bye.

185
00:08:45,280 --> 00:08:52,428
Bye.
